PROJECT OVERVIEW
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
Dataset:           500 streaming service customers
Overall Churn:     168 customers (33.6%)
Retention Rate:    332 customers (66.4%)
Analysis Method:   Statistical correlation analysis with hypothesis testing

ANALYSIS APPROACH
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
✓ Correlation analysis (Point-Biserial for continuous variables)
✓ Chi-square tests (for categorical variables)
✓ Distribution comparison (T-tests, median analysis)
✓ Segmentation analysis by variable levels
✓ Statistical significance testing (α = 0.05)

KEY FINDINGS SUMMARY
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ 🔴 PRIMARY DRIVER: CUSTOMER SERVICE INTERACTIONS (HIGHLY SIGNIFICANT)          │
├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│                                                                                 │
│ Statistical Strength:                                                           │
│  • Correlation coefficient: r = 0.5112 (Very Strong)                           │
│  • P-value: < 0.001 (Highly Significant)                                       │
│  • Statistical significance: ✓✓✓ CONFIRMED                                     │
│                                                                                 │
│ Quantitative Impact:                                                            │
│  • Retained customers average: 3.25 interactions                                │
│  • Churned customers average: 6.11 interactions                                │
│  • Difference: 2.86 more interactions (87% increase)                           │
│  • Median for retained: 3, Churned: 7                                          │
│                                                                                 │
│ Risk Stratification:                                                            │
│  0 interactions:      3.9% churn   (LOW - Baseline)                            │
│  1-2 interactions:    9.7% churn   (LOW)                                       │
│  3-4 interactions:   17.9% churn   (MEDIUM)                                    │
│  5-6 interactions:   43.9% churn   (HIGH) ⚠️                                    │
│  7-8 interactions:   67.3% churn   (CRITICAL) 🔴                               │
│                                                                                 │
│ Business Interpretation:                                                        │
│  High customer service interactions are a MAJOR RED FLAG indicating            │
│  unresolved issues, repeated support tickets, and customer frustration.         │
│  This is the STRONGEST predictor of churn in the dataset.                      │
│                                                                                 │
└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ ❌ NOT SIGNIFICANT: MONTHLY PRICE                                              │
├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ Correlation: r = 0.0290 (No correlation)                                       │
│ P-value: 0.518 (Not significant)                                               │
│ Retained avg: $12.97 | Churned avg: $13.23 | Difference: $0.25 (negligible)   │
│                                                                                 │
│ Conclusion: Price is NOT a churn driver.                                       │
│             Price reductions are unlikely to improve retention.                 │
│             Focus should be on service quality, not discounting.               │
└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ ❌ NOT SIGNIFICANT: TOTAL VIEWING HOURS (ENGAGEMENT)                           │
├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ Correlation: r = -0.0021 (No correlation)                                      │
│ P-value: 0.962 (Not significant)                                               │
│ Retained avg: 40.31 hours | Churned avg: 40.21 hours | Difference: 0.10      │
│                                                                                 │
│ Conclusion: Content engagement does NOT predict churn.                         │
│             Both high and low usage customers churn similarly.                  │
│             Problem is not content satisfaction.                                │
└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ ❌ NOT SIGNIFICANT: SUBSCRIPTION TIER                                          │
├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
