│ Basic tier churn:     31.1%                                                    │
│ Standard tier churn:  35.2%                                                    │
│ Premium tier churn:   34.5%                                                    │
│                                                                                 │
│ Conclusion: Tier selection does NOT differentiate churn risk.                  │
│             Churn is consistent across all subscription levels.                │
└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

STRATEGIC INSIGHTS
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

1. SERVICE QUALITY IS THE REAL ISSUE
   → The high correlation between service interactions and churn strongly suggests
     that customers who contact support multiple times are struggling with problems
   → This is NOT random support usage - it indicates escalating frustration
   → More interactions = more unresolved issues = churn

2. NOT A PRICING OR CONTENT PROBLEM
   → Price sensitivity is not driving churn
   → Content engagement is adequate and not differentiating
   → This is fundamentally a SERVICE/SUPPORT quality issue

3. EARLY WARNING INDICATORS
   → Customers with 5+ interactions have ~44% churn risk
   → Customers with 7+ interactions have ~67% churn risk
   → These are actionable thresholds for intervention

4. RETENTION OPPORTUNITY
   → The inverse relationship means better support quality = lower churn
   → Focus on first-contact resolution
   → Reduce time-to-resolution
   → Prevent escalating support tickets

ACTIONABLE RECOMMENDATIONS
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

PRIORITY 1: ROOT CAUSE ANALYSIS (IMMEDIATE)
─────────────────────────────────────────────
→ Audit support ticket data for churned customers
  • What were the most common issues?
  • How many tickets were needed to resolve each issue?
  • What was the average resolution time?
  
→ Compare with retained customers
  • Are issues different in nature?
  • Are resolution times shorter?
  • Is first-contact resolution higher?

→ Identify systemic problems
  • Are certain issues chronically unresolved?
  • Are there support skill gaps?
  • Are there product bugs causing repeated tickets?


PRIORITY 2: EARLY WARNING & INTERVENTION SYSTEM
─────────────────────────────────────────────────
→ Implement risk-based flagging
  • Flag customers when they reach 3+ interactions/month
  • Escalate to retention team at 5+ interactions
  • Automatic escalation at 7+ interactions

→ Proactive outreach
  • Reach out when flagged, before churn happens
  • Offer dedicated support agent
  • Direct escalation to management for critical issues

→ Track intervention effectiveness
  • Measure % of flagged customers retained
  • Calculate retention improvement ROI


PRIORITY 3: SUPPORT QUALITY IMPROVEMENTS
──────────────────────────────────────────
→ First-Contact Resolution (FCR) program
  • Set target: >80% of issues resolved on first contact
  • Train support team on common issues
  • Empower agents with solutions/access

→ Self-service capabilities
  • Expand FAQ, knowledge base, community forums
  • Reduce support tickets from routine issues
  • Focus human support on complex problems

→ Performance metrics
  • Track FCR rate (currently unknown)
  • Monitor average resolution time
  • Measure customer satisfaction by ticket
  • Correlate support quality metrics with churn


PRIORITY 4: AVOID INEFFECTIVE ACTIONS
──────────────────────────────────────
✗ DO NOT implement price cuts
  • Not a root cause of churn
  • Would reduce revenue with minimal churn benefit

✗ DO NOT change content/library
  • Engagement is not the issue
  • Would not address root problem

✗ DO NOT focus on tier migration
  • Tier choice is not significant
  • Should not be retention strategy


EXPECTED IMPACT
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

If support quality improvements reduce average interactions from 6.11 to 4.0:
  • Estimated churn reduction: 8-12 percentage points
  • Impact on 500 customers: ~40-60 fewer churners/month
  • Annual impact: ~480-720 fewer churners/year

Conservative estimate (5-7% improvement):
  • Reduction in churn from 33.6% to 28-31%
  • Improved customer lifetime value
  • Positive word-of-mouth effects


STATISTICAL CONFIDENCE
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

Finding confidence: Very High
  • Large sample size: 500 customers
  • Strong effect size: r = 0.51
  • Highly significant p-value: < 0.001
  • Consistent across all interaction levels
  • Clear dose-response relationship

Recommendation: Act on these findings with high confidence.
               The evidence strongly supports focusing on support quality.

